<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Zheng (Wang Shichong)</h1><p class="st-meta">619 CE – 621 CE · capital Luoyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The regime of the Sui general Wang Shichong at Luoyang, whose defeat by Li Shimin in 621 decided the struggle for the Sui succession in favour of the Tang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a>, a general of Central Asian descent, defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a> against the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-mi-sui-dynasty">Li Mi</a>. After the emperor's murder in 618 he and other officials enthroned the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-tong">Yang Tong</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> emperor; Wang crushed Li Mi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagang-army">Wagang army</a> and in 619 forced Yang Tong to abdicate, proclaiming the state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zheng">Zheng</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zheng">Zheng</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, but Wang governed through suspicion and harsh punishments, and the deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-tong">Yang Tong</a> was soon killed. Long sieges brought famine to Luoyang, and many officers defected to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a>. The regime had little time to build institutions of its own.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 620 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. After he defeated and captured the relieving army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hulao">Hulao</a> in 621, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a> surrendered. He was spared and sent into exile but murdered on the way by Dugu Xiude, whose father Wang had executed. His fall gave the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> the eastern heartland.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 619 CE – 621 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:72.02%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>619 CE – 621 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luoyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wang Shichong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zheng (619-621)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=620&amp;focus=zheng-wang-shichong" aria-label="Zheng (Wang Shichong) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7535 923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zheng (Wang Shichong) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7855 1179L7769 1191M7855 1179L7771 1192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Luoyang. <a href="/atlas?year=620&amp;focus=zheng-wang-shichong">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
